diagnosis_text:
A thorough inspection of every device in the dc_clos_bgp fabric finds no fault. All physical links are UP (leaf_router_0_0:eth0/1/2, leaf_router_0_1:eth0/1/2, both spines, super_spine — all interfaces reported UP with correct IP assignments). All five BGP sessions are in Established state with 0 dropped connections, and every router holds correct routing-table entries: leaf_router_0_0 has 10.0.1.0/24 via spine_router_0_0; leaf_router_0_1 has 10.0.0.0/24 via spine_router_0_0; both spines carry both host subnets; super_spine carries both subnets via spine_router_0_0. An ICMP test from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) returned 4/4 packets with 0% loss (~1.5 ms RTT), and a traceroute confirmed the full path: pc_0_0 → leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1 → pc_0_1 in exactly three routed hops (TTL=61). Startup frr.conf files on all five routers match their running configurations exactly, and pc host default gateways are correctly set (10.0.0.1 and 10.0.1.1 respectively). The BGP session uptime appearing short (~2 minutes at first inspection) is explained by the FRR log: all daemons performed their initial configuration read at 15:13:31 — this is simply fresh Kathara lab start-up, not a disruption. Root cause: there is no network anomaly; the helpdesk ticket's premise is incorrect. The fabric is fully operational, and the reported connectivity outage between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1 does not exist. No corrective action is needed; the user should verify their local client configuration or re-test connectivity.
=== END ===